WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
RLHF.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.567
Reinforcement learning from human feedback.

3
00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:07.100
You should be able to draw it on a whiteboard.

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.633
Here's the big picture.

5
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:14.433
First, humans rank pairs of model responses: this one is more helpful.

6
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:18.333
Next, a reward model is trained to predict those preferences.

7
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:24.233
Then the language model is optimized against that reward, using an algorithm called PPO.

8
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:27.400
Inside that last step is a loop.

9
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.567
The model generates a response.

10
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:31.433
The reward model scores it.

11
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:33.516
And PPO updates the model.

12
00:00:33.516 --> 00:00:40.167
A KL penalty keeps it close to a frozen reference copy, or it drifts into high scoring gibberish.

13
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:43.100
That means four models in memory.

14
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.500
The policy is the one being trained, and it generates.

15
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.800
The reward model scores the replies.

16
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.533
The value head estimates future reward.

17
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.300
And the frozen reference anchors the policy.

18
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.700
Here they are in code.

19
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.133
The four roles are named one per line.

20
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:05.300
The trainer takes a small coefficient that controls how strongly drift from the reference is penalized.

21
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.700
Why does this matter when you pick a model?

22
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.467
Its model card says aligned with RLHF, or DPO on a feedback dataset.

23
00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:18.067
That tells you it was optimized for ranked human preference.

24
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:23.633
Your flight ops users will notice it: concise, hedged, helpful sounding summaries.

25
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:25.593
Let's recap.

26
00:01:25.593 --> 00:01:29.200
Humans rank replies, and a reward model learns from them.

27
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.967
PPO optimizes the policy against that reward.

28
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:36.400
A KL penalty keeps the policy near its reference.

29
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.882
Four models make it expensive and finicky.

30
00:01:39.882 --> 00:01:40.867
Next up: DPO.
